{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01Rgcw21Qb5PGUC9ktM5344f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# 01. LLM Fundamentals — AI Engineering Interview Category\n2\t\n3\tCovers how large language models work — architecture, tokenization, embeddings, inference mechanics, and sampling — and why these fundamentals are prerequisite knowledge for every AI engineering role in 2026.\n4\t\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## Interview signals\n8\t\n9\t| You hear… | This category |\n10\t|-----------|---------------|\n11\t| \"How do transformers / self-attention work?\" | Architecture internals |\n12\t| \"What is tokenization and why does it matter?\" | Tokenization & cost |\n13\t| \"Explain embeddings and how they're used in RAG\" | Embeddings |\n14\t| \"What is KV cache and how does it help inference?\" | Inference optimization |\n15\t| \"Compare temperature, top-k, and top-p sampling\" | Decoding strategies |\n16\t| \"What are scaling laws and why do they matter?\" | Training fundamentals |\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## Mental model\n21\t\n22\tA strong candidate understands that an LLM is fundamentally a *next-token probability machine* built on the transformer's self-attention mechanism, trained at massive scale on next-token prediction. Weak candidates describe LLMs in vague terms (\"it understands language\") without grasping the mathematical core: query-key-value attention computes weighted sums over token representations, positional encodings inject order information, and autoregressive decoding samples from a learned distribution one token at a time. The critical engineering insight is that this architecture creates sharp resource constraints — inference is *memory-bandwidth-bound* (not compute-bound), KV cache size determines throughput, and tokenization choices directly set cost and context-window limits. Every production decision (caching, quantization, model tiering) flows from these fundamentals.\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## Sub-topics\n27\t\n28\t### 1. Transformer Architecture & Self-Attention\n29\t**When:** Technical screen opens with \"how do transformers work?\" or \"explain self-attention\"; asked at nearly every AI engineering interview at any level.\n30\t**What:** Self-attention computes relevance scores between all token pairs via dot-product Q·Kᵀ/√d_k, then uses those scores as weights over value vectors — enabling global context without the sequential bottleneck of RNNs."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa9f5373-3237-46e4-ac93-22e97ebb4ec1","uuid":"0edb5339-8301-433d-aad0-10fe04879830","timestamp":"2026-06-16T15:11:46.476Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/ai-engineering/categories/01-llm-fundamentals.md","content":"# 01. LLM Fundamentals — AI Engineering Interview Category\n\nCovers how large language models work — architecture, tokenization, embeddings, inference mechanics, and sampling — and why these fundamentals are prerequisite knowledge for every AI engineering role in 2026.\n\n---\n\n## Interview signals\n\n| You hear… | This category |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| \"How do transformers / self-attention work?\" | Architecture internals |\n| \"What is tokenization and why does it matter?\" | Tokenization & cost |\n| \"Explain embeddings and how they're used in RAG\" | Embeddings |\n| \"What is KV cache and how does it help inference?\" | Inference optimization |\n| \"Compare temperature, top-k, and top-p sampling\" | Decoding strategies |\n| \"What are scaling laws and why do they matter?\" | Training fundamentals |\n\n---\n\n## Mental model\n\nA strong candidate understands that an LLM is fundamentally a *next-token probability machine* built on the transformer's self-attention mechanism, trained at massive scale on next-token prediction. Weak candidates describe LLMs in vague terms (\"it understands language\") without grasping the mathematical core: query-key-value attention computes weighted sums over token representations, positional encodings inject order information, and autoregressive decoding samples from a learned distribution one token at a time. The critical engineering insight is that this architecture creates sharp resource constraints — inference is *memory-bandwidth-bound* (not compute-bound), KV cache size determines throughput, and tokenization choices directly set cost and context-window limits. Every production decision (caching, quantization, model tiering) flows from these fundamentals.\n\n---\n\n## Sub-topics\n\n### 1. Transformer Architecture & Self-Attention\n**When:** Technical screen opens with \"how do transformers work?\" or \"explain self-attention\"; asked at nearly every AI engineering interview at any level.\n**What:** Self-attention computes relevance scores between all token pairs via dot-product Q·Kᵀ/√d_k, then uses those scores as weights over value vectors — enabling global context without the sequential bottleneck of RNNs.","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":175}}}
